In article <1991Apr3.183713.11882@leland.Stanford.EDU> pippin@leland.Stanford.EDU (Michael Killianey) writes:
>
>Well, if all that we're concerned about is "unfair" scoring, how about
>making the DURATION of the game a more important factor.  Seems to me
>that most of the currently known "features" that players like to take
>advantage of cost lots and lots of time.
>
>Many video games have "time bonuses" if the player finishes within a
>certain time limit.  This could be extended to NetHack scores.  It'd
>be a little silly to demand that a player only use N turns to solve
>_NetHack_, but perhaps the bonus for achieving demigod(dess)hood could
>be inversely proportional to the time spent getting there.  So if I
>finish the endgame in N turns, I get twice the bonus of someone who
>finishes in 2N turns (probably due to the time they spent polypiling,
>dancing with trolls, etc.)  High score lists should note how many turns
>the game lasted.
>
>
>This only affects the highest scores on the list.  If your list
>doesn't have any demigod(desse)s, this might not be an adequate
>solution.  Maybe this "time bonus"/"time penalty" idea could be
>extended to incomplete games?  There could be some bonus based on
>lowest level reached, character exp points, number of turns taken (and
>maybe even the "nobility" of your cause of death, where dying of
>starvation or being killed by a grid bug would be low scoring and
>being killed by the wiz or a demon would be high scoring.)
>
>Dev team, I LIKE all of the shortcut "features" of NetHack.  They're
>fun to use, and it's nice to know that they exist.  I would hate to
>see these removed from the game...I know many players that would never
>get to see the castle if they couldn't pull these stunts.  Don't take
>away these fun parts of the game just for the sake of the integrity of
>the high score list.  If people don't like the "high score potential"
>of these tricks, please try to change the "high score list" criteria
>to make them less high scoring.  Just don't rob the game of these
>enchanting features.
>

Well, here I am agreeing with someone else's point again (or mooching
off someone else's input). I have advocated such a "time bonus/time
penalty" system (actually "_turn_ bonus/penalty") for both Moria and
NetHack; I think both games would benefit from it.
And for God's sake, don't do anything so silly as to gut the game of its
many "shortcuts;" they are a big part of its charm. Take them away and
you've just got a slightly more elaborate version of Moria. I can see
taking away things like that method posted on here to get an intrinsic
armor class of -127, and preventing people from getting unlimited
wishes, but please, Dev Team, be very careful about what you take out.


